CONSTRUCTOR BASICS — rules and features:

```java
public class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // CONSTRUCTOR: same name as class, NO return type
    public Account(String accountId, BigDecimal initialBalance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = initialBalance;
        // Called when: new Account("SAV2001", BigDecimal.ZERO)
    }

    // OVERLOADED constructor (multiple constructors):
    public Account(String accountId) {
        this(accountId, BigDecimal.ZERO);  // chains to first constructor
    }

    // NO-ARG constructor (if needed):
    public Account() {
        this("UNKNOWN", BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}

// Rules:
// 1. Same name as class
// 2. NO return type (not even void!)
// 3. Called automatically by 'new'
// 4. Can be overloaded (multiple constructors with different params)
// 5. If you write NONE → Java provides default no-arg constructor
// 6. If you write ANY constructor → Java does NOT provide default
```

PRIVATE CONSTRUCTOR USE CASE 1 — SINGLETON:

```java
// Only ONE instance of the RBI connection manager in the entire bank:
public class RbiConnectionManager {
    // THE single instance:
    private static final RbiConnectionManager INSTANCE = new RbiConnectionManager();

    // PRIVATE constructor — nobody outside can call 'new'!
    private RbiConnectionManager() {
        // Initialize connection pool, SSL certificates, etc.
        System.out.println("RBI connection initialized (happens ONCE)");
    }

    // Public access to the single instance:
    public static RbiConnectionManager getInstance() {
        return INSTANCE;  // always returns the SAME object
    }

    public String sendReport(String report) {
        return "Sent to RBI: " + report;
    }
}

// Usage:
RbiConnectionManager mgr1 = RbiConnectionManager.getInstance();
RbiConnectionManager mgr2 = RbiConnectionManager.getInstance();
// mgr1 == mgr2 → true! Same instance!
// new RbiConnectionManager() → COMPILE ERROR! Constructor is private!

// Modern alternative: enum Singleton
public enum RbiConnection {
    INSTANCE;
    public String sendReport(String report) { return "Sent: " + report; }
}
// RbiConnection.INSTANCE.sendReport("report") — thread-safe, serialization-safe
```

PRIVATE CONSTRUCTOR USE CASE 2 — FACTORY METHOD:

```java
// Factory methods provide NAMED construction — clearer than 'new'
public class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    private final String type;
    private final BigDecimal balance;
    private final BigDecimal interestRate;

    // PRIVATE constructor — callers MUST use factory methods
    private Account(String accountId, String type, BigDecimal balance, BigDecimal rate) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.type = type;
        this.balance = balance;
        this.interestRate = rate;
    }

    // FACTORY METHODS — named, self-documenting:
    public static Account createSavings(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        return new Account(id, "SAVINGS", balance, new BigDecimal("0.04"));
        // Name says: "I'm creating a SAVINGS account"
    }

    public static Account createCurrent(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        return new Account(id, "CURRENT", balance, BigDecimal.ZERO);
        // Name says: "I'm creating a CURRENT account"
    }

    public static Account createNRI(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        return new Account(id, "NRI", balance, new BigDecimal("0.035"));
    }
}

// Usage — CLEAR and self-documenting:
Account savings = Account.createSavings("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"));
Account current = Account.createCurrent("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("850000"));

// vs confusing constructor call:
// Account a = new Account("SAV2001", "SAVINGS", new BigDecimal("125000"), new BigDecimal("0.04"));
// What does each parameter mean? Which type? What rate? UNCLEAR!
```

PRIVATE CONSTRUCTOR USE CASE 3 — UTILITY CLASS:

```java
// All-static utility class — NO reason to create instances
public final class InterestCalculator {

    // PRIVATE constructor — prevents instantiation
    private InterestCalculator() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Utility class — do not instantiate!");
        // Even reflection can't create it — throws exception!
    }

    // All methods are STATIC:
    public static BigDecimal simpleInterest(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal rate, int years) {
        return principal.multiply(rate).multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(years));
    }

    public static BigDecimal compoundInterest(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal rate, int years) {
        return principal.multiply(BigDecimal.ONE.add(rate).pow(years)).subtract(principal);
    }
}

// Usage — only static methods:
BigDecimal si = InterestCalculator.simpleInterest(new BigDecimal("100000"), new BigDecimal("0.04"), 5);
// new InterestCalculator() → COMPILE ERROR! Constructor is private.
// Even if accessed via reflection → UnsupportedOperationException!
```

